= State continuity (technical) =

== Operational model ==
Let a system have state S_t and a transition relation T. A successor S_(t+1) may result from T(S_t, input, environment). A claim of continuity additionally needs an identity policy, provenance and allowed transition rules; it is not entailed by equality of state representations.

<pre>
identity_policy(I_t, I_(t+1), event) -> preserve | derive | fork | replace
transition(S_t, input, event) -> S_(t+1)
evidence = {source revision, actor/principal, event, integrity proof, time/ordering}
</pre>

These expressions are a proposed modeling vocabulary, not a universally standardized identity calculus.

== Checkpoint, log and distributed execution ==
A process snapshot is insufficient for a system with in-flight messages unless the checkpoint records a consistent cut. The Chandy–Lamport distributed snapshot algorithm supplies a foundational method for recording a consistent global state in an asynchronous system under its assumed channel behavior. Crash recovery, exactly-once effects and authorization still require additional mechanisms.

Versioned snapshots and event-sourced transitions serve distinct purposes: snapshots accelerate restoration; events explain how a successor was derived. A cryptographic hash can support integrity, but equality of hashes does not prove ownership, consciousness, or authority.

== Forking and restoration ==
A restored state may create a new branch or replica. Explicitly distinguish continuity of a particular operational subject, derivation from source state and equivalence of data. For agents, resolve the persistent identity and permissions before accepting imported memories or goals.

== Biology and philosophy boundary ==
Genetic descent supplies a lineage relation, not numerical identity; psychological memory may support but does not conclusively determine identity in branching thought experiments. Neither can be substituted for a software consistency protocol without an explicit analogy boundary.

